The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|cosin}}, {{m|enm|cosine}}, {{m|enm|cosyn|t=blood relative, kinsman or kinswoman; any relative; nephew or niece; first cousin; grandson or granddaughter; descendant; godchild or godparent, or a relative of a godchild or godparent; (''figurative'') closely related or similar thing}}{{nb...|coosin, cosen, coseyn, cosigne, cosing, cossen, cossin, cossigne, cossine, cossyn, cossyne, cosyne, cosynne, cousin, cousine, cousyn, cozyn, cusin, cusine, cussyn, cusyn, kosen, kosin, kosyn|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=cǒsī̆n(e, cọ̄sī̆n(e|pos=n|id=MED9877}}</ref> and then:

* from {{der|en|xno|cosen}}, {{m|xno|cosin}}{{nb...|cosigne, cosine, cousin|otherforms=1}}, {{der|en|frm|cosin}}, and {{der|en|fro|cosin|t=collateral male relative more distant than one’s brother; form of address used by a monarch to male monarchs or nobles}} {{...|cousin, cusin|otherforms=1}} (modern {{cog|fr|cousin}}); and
* from {{der|en|xno|cosine}}, {{der|en|frm|cosine}}, and {{der|en|fro|cosine|t=collateral female relative more distant than one’s sister; form of address used by a monarch to female monarchs or nobles}} {{...|cousine, cusine|otherforms=1}} (modern {{cog|fr|cousine}}),

from {{der|en|la|cōnsobrīnus|t=maternal cousin; first cousin; relation}} (possibly through {{der|en|VL.|*cōsuīnus}}, from {{m|VL.|*cōsobīnus}}), from {{m|la|con-|pos={{glossary|prefix}} denoting a bringing together of several objects}} + {{m|la|sobrīnus|t=maternal cousin; sister’s son; any nephew}} (from a noun use of {{der|en|itc-pro|*swezrīnos|t=of or belonging to a sister|pos=adjective}} (with the first syllable influenced by {{cog|la|soror|t=sister}}), ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*swésōr|t=sister}}, possibly from {{m|ine-pro|*swé|t=self}} + {{m|ine-pro|*h₁ésh₂r̥|t=blood}} (that is, a woman of one’s own blood) or {{m|ine-pro|*-sōr|pos={{glossary|feminine}} {{glossary|suffix}}}}).<ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=n.'' and ''adj|id=5062644066|date=September 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref>

The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from the noun.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=5407414517|date=July 2023}}</ref>

