##* {{quote-journal|en|title=A Winter in the South|journal=[[w:Harper's Magazine|Harper’s New Monthly Magazine]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Harper (publisher)|Harper & Brothers]],{{nb...|publishers, 327 to 335 Pearl Street, Franklin Square.}}|month=September|year=1857|volume=XV|issue=LXXXVIII|pages=441–442|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=fkJGAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA442|oclc=924884025|passage=At length she seemed to relent, or changed her tactics, for she looked over his shoulder as he sketched, and '''Cousined''' him two or three times as usual.}}
##* {{RQ:Newberry Odd One|chapter=Some of Beth's Friends|page=29|passage=Though he called Mrs. Merrit aunt, and '''cousined''' all her daughters, he was really no relative whatever. His father dying when he was a small boy, he had been kindly adopted by that father's step-brother, who had married into the Merritt family.}}
## {{lb|en|also|reflexive}} To [[regard#Verb|regard]] (oneself or someone) as a cousin to another [[person#Noun|person]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=G. Herbert Rodwell|title=The Chimney Piece. A Farce, in One Act.{{nb...|[...] Performed at the Theatre Royal Drury Lane.}}|series=Miller’s Modern Acting Drama,{{nb...|Consisting of the Most Popular Pieces Produced at the London Theatres, Subject to the Provisions of the Dramatic Copyright Act.}}|seriesvolume=no. 5|location=London|publisher=John Miller,{{nb...|Henrietta Street, Covent Garden, (agent to the Dramatic Authors Society.)}}|year=1833|section=scene i|page=2|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=3hraJKbdgVQC&pg=RA5-PA2|oclc=1070216348|passage={{smallcaps|Mrs. M[uddlebrain].}} {{...}} Mary, who is this young man? / {{smallcaps|Mary.}} That's my cousin, ma'am, just stept in to lend us a helping hand in placing the things. / {{...}} / {{smallcaps|Shuffle.}} What the devil did she say about a tall grenadier, and the pantry? Mrs. Shuffle! Mrs. Shuffle! / {{smallcaps|Mary.}} Hush! Are you mad? Do you want to tell all the world that we're married, and get me turned away? / {{smallcaps|Shuffle.}} No; but the grenadier? / {{smallcaps|Mary.}} Came to see the cook; so to prevent all the fat being in the fire, I '''cousined''' him, and made him a relation. / {{smallcaps|Shuffle.}} Yes; and remember you've '''cousined''' me too.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=J[acob] Sam[ue]l Vandersloot|quotee=Cyrus Sturdivant|chapter=‘To God be All Praise’|title=The True Path; or, The Murphy Movement and Gospel Temperance.{{nb...|A Complete History of the Rise, Progress and Wonderful Effects of the Great Reformatory Wave Now Deluging Our Land; together with the Biography, Addresses, Incidents and Anecdotes of Francis Murphy, the Great Temperance Apostle. The Most Telling Speeches and Appeals of Prominent Leaders and Helpers in Various Parts, with the Intensely Interesting Experiences of the Most Remarkable of the Reformed Men.}}|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=William Flint,{{nb...|Philadelphia. Haber Brothers, Minneapolis, Minn. H. H. Natt, St. Louis, Mo.}}|date=28 May 1877|year_published=1877|page=244|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=yXnaAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA244|oclc=55118761|passage=[T]he old gentleman took me into the house and introduced me to the family, where I was at once '''cousined''' by them all.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Scotigena Oxoniensis [pseudonym]|title=London. I. The Row and Westminster. Epistle to a Friend.|journal=[[w:Blackwood's Magazine|Blackwood’s Edinburgh Magazine]]|location=Edinburgh; London|publisher=[[w:Blackwood (publishing house)|William Blackwood & Sons]],{{nb...|Edinburgh; and 37 Paternoster Row, London.}}|month=July|year=1885|volume=CXXXVIII|issue=DCCCXXXVII|page=135|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=rDRPAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA135|oclc=1042815524|passage=[A] maiden well braced in nerve and muscle, / Far from sensual ease, to be mother of lustiest Britons, / '''Cousined''' to Romans in strength and in breadth of masterful Empire.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|translator=[[w:John Stuart Blackie|[John Stuart] Blackie]]|title=The Old Owl of the Sron|editor=[[w:Alexander Mackenzie (historian)|Alexander Mackenzie]]|journal=The Celtic Magazine:{{nb...|A Monthly Periodical Devoted to the Literature, History, Antiquities, Folk Lore, Traditions, and the Social and Material Interests of the Celt at Home and Abroad.}}|location=Inverness, Inverness-shire|publisher=A[lexander] & W. Mackenzie,{{nb...|47 High Street.}}|month=September|year=1885|volume=X|issue=CXIX|page=522|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=b3k5AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA522|oclc=7907922|passage=O Donald, thou wert the boy, / Steel to the bone, and like thee none! / '''Cousined''' wert thou to the great Clan Chattan, / Thou, the nodding cliff's foster son.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Steinbeck|chapter=|title=[[w:Travels with Charley|Travels with Charley in Search of America]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Viking Press|year=1962|year_published=March 1968|page=201|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/travelswithcharl0000john_e6e2/page/201/mode/1up|oclc=1408541305|passage=Let me say in the beginning that even if I wanted to avoid Texas I could not, for I am wived in Texas and mother-in-lawed and uncled and aunted and '''cousined''' within an inch of my life.}}
