## To [[associate#Verb|associate]] with someone or something on a [[close#Adjective|close]] [[basis]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Garrett Stewart|chapter=Modernism and the Flicker Effect|title=Between Film and Screen: Modernism’s Photo Synthesis|location=Chicago, Ill.; London|publisher=w:University of Chicago Press|year=1999|page=310|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=PcCVvc0DQH4C&pg=PA310|isbn=978-0-226-77411-4|passage=In an appendix to ''The Mechanic Muse'', he &#91;{{w|Hugh Kenner}}&#93; finds Victorian symbolist practice serving to release the signifier from centuries of post-Enlightenment confusion about the proper wedding (or at least '''cousining''') of word and thing.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Caperton Tissot|quotee=Elise Chapin|chapter=Some Offered Healing, Some Found Healing|editor=Willem Tissot|title=History between the Lines: Women’s Lives and Saranac Lake Customs|location=Jay, N.Y.|publisher=Graphics North|year=2007|pages=110–111|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Qgsp8MQRQdQC&pg=PA110|isbn=978-0-9643452-9-4|passage=[P]atients would escape into the town for a bit of a fling or "'''cousining'''" as it was called. "'''Cousining'''" was a Saranac Lake euphemism that applied to a couple, both of them patients and sometimes already married with a spouse living far away, who spent time together or dated each other.|footer={{small|A noun use.}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=David Roche; Bob McKee|chapter=The Moral Failure of Democracy|title=Democrisis: Democracy Caused the Debt Crisis. Will It Survive It?|location=[London]|publisher=Independent Strategy|year=2012|pages=12–13|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=4_puDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA13|isbn=978-1-4717-5165-3|passage=The UK has fiscal arithmetic '''cousined''' with that of Greece, but is dealing with it.}}
## To [[visit#Verb|visit]] a cousin or other [[relation]].
##* {{quote-journal|en|title=A Chapter on Cousins|journal=w:Dublin University Magazine|location=Dublin|publisher=William Curry, Jun. and Company; London: [[w:Simpkin & Marshall|Simpkin, Marshall, and Co.]]|month=July|year=1836|volume=VIII|issue=XLIII|page=28|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=W1pRAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA28|column=1|oclc=5793274|passage=You know when you get up in the morning that you have a certain quantity of '''cousining''' to go through before the day is over, and you make up your mind to it; read a page of {{w|Seneca}}, add a verse to your litany, and commit yourself to Providence, like a wise man and a Christian.|footer={{small|A noun use.}}}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=B. C. True|title=Cousining in Autumn|editors=Thomas L. Harris; John Tanner|journal=The Gavel: A Monthly Periodical Devoted to [[w:Independent Order of Odd Fellows|Odd Fellowship]] and General Literature|location=Albany, N.Y.|publisher=John Tanner|date=20 October 1845|year_published=November 1845|volume=II|issue=3|page=80|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=m9kcAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA80|oclc=1570464|passage=Who then that has a cousin, has aught to say against '''cousining'''? We do indeed often her sneeringly the expression of "Dutch '''cousining'''" or "Yankee '''cousining'''," as if there was something mean in the act of visiting those who are "next of kin." To such as do it, I feel an unconquerable aversion or excessive pity; as they appear censorious or betray a stupidity that cannot feel a consanguine tie beyond their hearth.|footer={{small|A noun use.}}}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Herminius Cobb|title=[The Household.] Mr. Blossom Visits His Relations.|journal=The American Magazine: Supplement|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=The American Magazine Company,{{nb...|749 Broadway.}}; London: The Christian Million Company|month=June|year=1887|volume=I (New Series; volume VI overall)|issue=2|page=245|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=mZ48AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA245|column=1|oclc=2444700|passage=It isn't the thing for a man to be like a stranger to his own flesh and blood. I'm going '''cousining''', Sue, down East, and I'll hunt up my relations.}}
##* {{RQ:Parker Seats of the Mighty|chapter=As Vain as Absalom|page=87|passage=The pretty wren perches now in the Governor's house—a-'''cousining''', a-'''cousining'''.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|title=An 80th Wedding Anniversary|editor=[[w:Henry Luce|Henry R[obinson] Luce]]|journal=[[w:Life (magazine)|Life]]|location=Chicago, Ill.; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Time Inc.}}|date=5 January 1959|volume=46|issue=1|page=78|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Gj8EAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA78|issn=0024-3019|oclc=34142982|passage=In 1878 they were married in the Mormon Temple in St. George, 300 miles away, and he drove her back home in a hay wagon in eight days. They "'''cousined'''" (stopped with relatives) all the way.}}

